Give the gradient stop order a consistent rule

GradientStop::operator< tests the position with an exact comparison and
then falls back to the equality of qtcEqual, which holds for a
difference below 0.0001. Two stops that sit closer than that compare
less than each other in both directions, so the order is not a strict
weak ordering.

GradientStopCont derives from std::set, and the reader accepts any
position between zero and one, so a file reaches that state. The
container then breaks. A pair of near positions traverses in falling
order. A lookup then misses a stop that the set holds. Both the writer
and the paint code walk the stops in traversal order.

Order the stops on the grid of qtcEqual. Each field goes to its nearest
grid point, so two stops on one point are equivalent and the order obeys
the rule of std::set. The epsilon takes a name, because the equality and
the order must hold one value.

The grid also repairs a lookup of the config dialog. The dialog rebuilds
a stop from the text of its row. That text holds six significant digits.
A rebuilt position of 0.984 therefore differs from the stored position
by one unit in the last place. The old rule tests the position exactly
first and misses such a stop, and an exact rule misses it as well.

The built-in gradients and every file that the dialog wrote keep their
exact text, because their positions stand 0.001 apart at least.

The change sits on the branch work/config-io of the local tree, as commit 17 of 18.
A new merge request on system/qtcurve takes that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

diff --git a/lib/utils/number.h b/lib/utils/number.h
index 7e66b66b..89f359cc 100644
--- a/lib/utils/number.h
+++ b/lib/utils/number.h
@@ -47,7 +47,9 @@ qtcSquare(const T &a)
 
 #define qtcBound(a, b, c) qtcMax(a, qtcMin(b, c))
 #define qtcLimit(v, l) qtcBound(0, v, l)
-#define qtcEqual(v1, v2) (std::abs(v1 - v2) < 0.0001)
+// qtcEqual calls two numbers equal when the difference is below this value.
+#define QTC_EQUAL_EPSILON 0.0001
+#define qtcEqual(v1, v2) (std::abs(v1 - v2) < QTC_EQUAL_EPSILON)
 
 namespace QtCurve {
 
diff --git a/qt6/common/common.h b/qt6/common/common.h
index b6c04125..4b642b2b 100644
--- a/qt6/common/common.h
+++ b/qt6/common/common.h
@@ -660,16 +660,25 @@ struct GradientStop {
                 qtcEqual(alpha, o.alpha));
     }
 
+    // Order two stops on the grid of qtcEqual. Two stops on one grid point
+    // are equivalent, so the order is a strict weak ordering, the rule that
+    // std::set demands of its comparison.
     bool operator<(const GradientStop &o) const
     {
-        return pos<o.pos || (qtcEqual(pos, o.pos) &&
-                             (val < o.val || (qtcEqual(val, o.val) &&
-                                              alpha < o.alpha)));
+        if(grid(pos)!=grid(o.pos))
+            return grid(pos)<grid(o.pos);
+        if(grid(val)!=grid(o.val))
+            return grid(val)<grid(o.val);
+        return grid(alpha)<grid(o.alpha);
     }
 
     double pos,
            val,
            alpha;
+
+private:
+    // Give the number of the nearest point of the grid of qtcEqual.
+    static long grid(double v) { return std::lround(v/QTC_EQUAL_EPSILON); }
 };
 
 typedef struct
